#---
# purpose: "extern float lanes: a float argument and a float return travel in the float register bank, on every door"
#---
#
# # extern_float_lanes
#
# Integers and floats do not travel in the same registers. The C ABI passes
# integers in one bank and floats in another, so both the caller and the callee
# have to agree on which bank each argument and the return value use. Nothing
# checked that agreement, and each door got it wrong differently:
#
#     extern "C" def libc::sqrt(float) :: float
#     libc::sqrt(9.0)        # 3.0
#
#   interp -> 9.0    the argument was handed over as its bits in the INTEGER
#                    bank, where sqrt never looks; the answer was then read out
#                    of the integer return register, which still held those same
#                    bits. It echoed its input, which is the worst possible
#                    wrong answer: it looks like a plausible result.
#   run    -> Cranelift verification failure, because the f64 the extern
#            returned was labelled a tagged value ref and then unboxed.
#   build  -> the same.
#
# ## What it covers
#
# Every assignment of the two banks that fz can express in one or two
# parameters, because the bug is exactly a bank disagreement:
#
#   * `(float) :: float`            -- float in, float out
#   * `(float, float) :: float`     -- two floats
#   * `(float, integer) :: float`   -- MIXED, and
#   * `(integer, float) :: float`   -- MIXED THE OTHER WAY ROUND. Both, because
#                                      one alone cannot tell a correct table
#                                      from one with the two mixed shapes
#                                      transposed: each would still answer its
#                                      own case and fail only the other.
#   * `(cstring) :: float`          -- integer bank in, float bank out
#   * `(integer) :: integer`        -- the all-integer control, which always
#                                      worked and must keep working
#
# These are real libc functions with exact binary results at these inputs, so
# the expected output is not platform-sensitive: every value here is a small
# power of two or an exact sum of them.
extern "C" def libc::sqrt(float) :: float
extern "C" def libc::pow(float, float) :: float
extern "C" def libc::ldexp(float, integer) :: float
extern "C" def libc::jn(integer, float) :: float
extern "C" def libc::atof(cstring) :: float
extern "C" def libc::abs(integer) :: integer

def main() do
  dbg(libc::sqrt(16.0))
  dbg(libc::pow(2.0, 10.0))
  dbg(libc::ldexp(1.5, 3))
  dbg(libc::jn(0, 0.0))
  dbg(libc::atof("2.5"))
  dbg(libc::abs(-42))
end
